1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The Practical Pig is a animation film directed by Dick Rickard.

2
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
After his two brothers are captured, the third little pig
invents a machine to capture the big bad wolf.

3
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Big Bad Wolf (voice) (uncredited), played by Billy Bletcher.

4
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Fiddler Pig (voice) (uncredited), played by Mary Moder.

5
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Fifer Pig (voice) (uncredited), played by Dorothy Compton.

6
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Practical Pig (voice) (uncredited), played by Pinto Colvig.

7
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
(voice) (uncredited), played by Tommy Wiggins.

8
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
(voice) (uncredited), played by Tom Buchanan.

9
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
The Practical Pig is a Silly Symphonies animated
short film produced by Walt Disney.

10
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
It was released on February 24, 1939, and was directed by Dick Rickard.

11
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
It was the fourth and final cartoon starring The Three Pigs.

12
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Like its predecessors, The Practical Pig incorporates the
song "Who's Afraid of the Big Bad Wolf?".

13
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Unlike its predecessors however, its title cards labeled it as a standalone Three Little
Pigs cartoon, suggesting that they were to get their own series of cartoons.

14
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
It is also the second-to-last Silly Symphony released.

15
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:36,000
The film explores themes of wolf, pig, kidnapping, spanking, swimming.
